iding sequences  to the  team at uh that was building the axiom  array  and and they also then applied analyses  and gave us back scores  and i think that was a really great  partnership because between the two  bioinformatics teams  we ended up with uh with a really  excellent design  it was a little nerve-racking when we  were doing this because we were  designing this  and you know of course bioinformatically  and then sending the design off  and we that those were anxious moments  for us will this how will this go for us  so we started with the 850 k snip array  tremendous um exciting thing for us  because we laid probes down every 0.1  of of the genome uh and and  uh just waited for the data to come back  and one of the very first things we did  was use that data  to look at population structure and i'll  just cut right to the chase of what  happened now the 850  000 snips we easily got about a half a  million  that were really strongly co-dominant  the other 400 000 or so were were fine  too there were several that  had no minor allele homozygotes that  type of thing  so we really focused in on that half a  million it was way more than we really  needed  for most of our applications but as the  studies came together we we knew a lot  about population structure  and we found that we got excellent  concordance  from the array to whole genome shotgun  sequencing patterns of diversity  so that i i don't want to go through the  the whole story of what's happening in  these  patterns of diversity it was just really  a message of saying  that one concern people always have  about  array technology is a snip ascertainment  bias  and we certainly have it we design the  array to  capture alleles that were found in the  global domesticated population  uh but what we see is a really  uh powerful tool to uh  tell the story of domestication of  strawberry and to navigate  uh through that complex genome the other  thing that's been incredibly powerful  for us in in  is build